**Theorem.** Let $G$ be a finite group with exactly $n$ elements of order $p$, where $p$ is prime. Then either $n = 0$ or $p \mid (n+1)$.

**Proof.**

If $n = 0$ there is nothing to prove. Suppose $n > 0$. Then $G$ contains an element of order $p$, so by Lagrange's theorem $p \mid |G|$.

Let $\mathcal{S}$ be the set of subgroups of $G$ of order $p$. Every subgroup $H \in \mathcal{S}$ has exactly $p - 1$ elements of order $p$ (its non-identity elements). Since $p$ is prime, any two distinct subgroups of order $p$ intersect trivially: a non-identity element in the intersection would generate both, forcing them to be equal. Therefore the elements of order $p$ are partitioned among the members of $\mathcal{S}$, giving

$$n = (p-1)\,|\mathcal{S}|. \tag{1}$$

It suffices to show $|\mathcal{S}| \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$, for then

$$n + 1 = (p-1)|\mathcal{S}| + 1 \equiv (-1)(1) + 1 = 0 \pmod{p}.$$

**Claim.** $|\mathcal{S}| \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$.

*Proof of Claim.* Pick $P \in \mathcal{S}$ and let $P$ act on $\mathcal{S}$ by conjugation. Each orbit has size dividing $|P| = p$, hence is either $1$ or $p$. Therefore

$$|\mathcal{S}| \equiv |\operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S})| \pmod{p},$$

where $\operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S}) = \{Q \in \mathcal{S} : P \leq N_G(Q)\}$.

Clearly $P \in \operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S})$ since $P$ normalizes itself. We analyze the remaining fixed points. Let $Q \in \operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S})$ with $Q \neq P$. Then $P \cap Q = \{e\}$ (distinct prime-order subgroups), so $PQ$ is a subgroup of $G$ of order $p^2$. Being a $p$-group of order $p^2$, it is abelian: its center is nontrivial (center of a $p$-group), and if $|Z(PQ)| = p$ then $PQ/Z(PQ)$ is cyclic of order $p$, forcing $PQ$ abelian — a contradiction; hence $Z(PQ) = PQ$.

Since $PQ$ is abelian of order $p^2$ and contains two distinct subgroups of order $p$ ($P$ and $Q$), it is not cyclic, so $PQ \cong \mathbb{Z}_p \times \mathbb{Z}_p$. This group has exactly $p + 1$ subgroups of order $p$, and because $PQ$ is abelian, $P$ centralizes — hence normalizes — each of them. Thus all $p + 1$ subgroups of order $p$ in $PQ$ lie in $\operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S})$.

Define a relation on $\operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S}) \setminus \{P\}$ by $Q_1 \sim Q_2 \iff PQ_1 = PQ_2$. This is an equivalence relation, and each class consists of the $p$ subgroups of order $p$ in $PQ$ other than $P$ itself. (If two abelian $p^2$-subgroups $PQ_1, PQ_2$ share a subgroup $R \neq P$ of order $p$, then both equal $PR$, so the classes are disjoint.) Hence

$$|\operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S}) \setminus \{P\}| = p \cdot m \quad\text{for some } m \geq 0,$$

and therefore

$$|\operatorname{Fix}_P(\mathcal{S})| = 1 + pm \equiv 1 \pmod{p}.$$

Consequently $|\mathcal{S}| \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$, proving the Claim. $\square$

Combining the Claim with equation (1):

$$n + 1 = (p-1)|\mathcal{S}| + 1 \equiv (p-1)\cdot 1 + 1 = p \equiv 0 \pmod{p}.$$

Thus $p \mid (n+1)$, completing the proof. $\blacksquare$

